# Triage Factory — multi-mode environment template.
# Copy to .env, fill in the values below, then:
#   ./triagefactory jwk-init --write-env .env   # populates GOTRUE_JWT_KEYS, GOTRUE_JWT_SECRET, TF_GOTRUE_SERVICE_ROLE_TOKEN
#   docker compose up -d
#
# Required values are left blank; docker-compose.yml uses ${VAR:?} on each
# so an unfilled .env fails fast at `docker compose up` rather than booting
# a stack with placeholder credentials.

# Postgres superuser password. Used for migrations and admin tasks; will
# NOT be the password TF's app pool connects with (the `authenticator`
# role below has its own password).
#
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# (NOT base64 — docker-compose.yml interpolates this directly into the
# URL-form TF_DATABASE_URL DSN. Base64's `/`, `+`, `=` are URL-reserved
# and would break pgx's connection-string parser; hex avoids the whole
# class of issues. The interpolation happens before TF sees the DSN,
# so url.UserPassword can't encode away the problem.)
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# Password for the `supabase_auth_admin` role (GoTrue's DB user). Kept
# distinct from POSTGRES_PASSWORD so a GoTrue compromise doesn't surrender
# superuser credentials. Set this before first `docker compose up`; the
# `postgres-postinit` sidecar reconciles this role password on each
# `docker compose up`, so updates here can be applied after first boot too.
#
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# (NOT base64 — GoTrue's DB lib requires URL-form connection strings;
# hex avoids every URL-reserved character by construction.)
SUPABASE_AUTH_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Password for the `authenticator` role — the role TF's app pool
# connects as for RLS-active request handling. Kept distinct from
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD and SUPABASE_AUTH_ADMIN_PASSWORD so a compromise
# of one pool doesn't surrender the others. The postgres-postinit
# sidecar applies this on every `docker compose up` via
# `ALTER USER authenticator WITH PASSWORD`.
#
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# (Hex is recommended for consistency with the other role passwords.
# The TF binary builds the app DSN via net/url.UserPassword which
# percent-encodes safely, so this one isn't strictly bound by the
# URL-reserved-char constraint that POSTGRES_PASSWORD and
# SUPABASE_AUTH_ADMIN_PASSWORD are — but using hex everywhere keeps
# rotation runbooks uniform.)
TF_AUTHENTICATOR_PASSWORD=

# Public-facing URL of this deployment. Used for OAuth redirect URIs,
# GoTrue issuer claim, and the cookie domain (D7). No trailing slash.
TF_PUBLIC_URL=

# Onboarding: whether self-service users may create their own org. Org
# creation is always a deliberate action — a fresh user lands at the
# "create your organization / wait for an invite" onboarding entry.
# Unset/false (default) leaves the "create" affordance enabled (right for
# hosted SaaS + open self-hosts). Set true on a locked-down self-host to
# disable self-service creation, leaving only the invite path (users wait
# for an admin to add them). Boolean: true/false (case-insensitive and
# whitespace-tolerant, unlike TF_MODE). Local mode ignores it.
# TF_PREVENT_ORG_CREATION=false

# How many delegated agent runs may execute concurrently on this host.
# Default 4 — deliberately conservative (concurrency is also an API-spend
# throttle, so raising it raises your parallel token burn). Capacity is
# memory-bound and linear, measured at fleet scale with the real agent
# engine: budget ~0.25 GB of host RAM per concurrent run (~155 MB engine
# marginal — read-only engine pages are shared across sandboxes — plus
# supervisor and transcript headroom) and reserve ~12 GB for the platform:
#
#   runs ≈ (RAM − 12 GB) ÷ 0.25 GB      e.g. 64 GB host → ~200 runs
#
# See docs/sandbox-bench.md for the measurements. At boot TF logs this
# host's derived capacity and warns when the cap exceeds it. Values above
# 256 clamp to 256: each run owns a private /24 subnet out of one /16, so
# more can never exist on a host. Invalid values fall back to the default
# with a boot warning rather than failing the deploy.
# TF_MAX_CONCURRENT_RUNS=4

# Dispatch memory guardrail — the runtime companion to the cap above.
# When host MemAvailable drops below this floor (MiB), the dispatcher
# stops claiming queued runs; they stay queued and dispatch resumes on
# its own once memory recovers. This is what stops a cap set too high
# for the host from dispatching straight into the OOM killer. Default
# 4096. Set 0 to disable. Fails open when the host can't report
# memory (non-Linux dev boxes). Invalid values fall back to the
# default with a boot warning rather than failing the deploy.
# TF_DISPATCH_MEM_FLOOR_MB=4096

# Per-run memory ceiling (MiB). Each delegated run's gVisor sandbox is
# placed in its own cgroup with memory.max set to this value (swap
# pinned to 0), so one pathological run — a runaway build, a tool loop —
# OOM-kills alone instead of degrading every other run on the host. The
# run fails with a clear "exceeded its memory limit" error. Default
# 4096: deliberately ~16x the per-run planning budget, because real
# builds legitimately spike; this is host protection, not budget
# enforcement. Set 0 to disable. Requires `cgroup: private` on the
# triagefactory service (see docker-compose.yml); hosts where cgroup
# delegation isn't available fail open (runs proceed without ceilings,
# one boot warning).
# TF_RUN_MEMORY_LIMIT_MB=4096

# Privilege separation: route the sandbox's netns/iptables/cgroup/
# rootfs setup through a separate `cap-broker` process, and drop the
# orchestrator's own capabilities entirely at exec (docker/entrypoint.sh).
# Default ON in the provided container image — the orchestrator boots
# with an empty capability set; only the cap-broker holds
# CAP_SYS_ADMIN/CAP_NET_ADMIN. Set to a falsy value as a temporary
# rollback to the prior in-process, single-privileged-process behavior.
# Multi mode + Linux only; local mode ignores this. See docs/usage.md.
# Boolean (case-insensitive: true/false, yes/no, on/off, 1/0).
# TF_PRIVSEP=true

# Advanced / rarely overridden: the uid+gid the orchestrator drops to
# (docker/entrypoint.sh) and the cap-broker's control socket path.
# Defaults match the image's baked-in `tf-orchestrator` user and
# /run/tf/cap-broker.sock — override only for a custom base image or an
# unusual /run layout.
# TF_ORCHESTRATOR_UID=10001
# TF_ORCHESTRATOR_GID=10001
# TF_CAPBROKER_SOCKET=/run/tf/cap-broker.sock

# Trusted-proxy allowlist for client-IP extraction. clientIP feeds session
# forensics (sessions.ip_addr), the SOC2 auth audit log
# (auth_events.ip_address), and the pre-auth per-IP rate limiter — a
# security control. X-Forwarded-For is client-appended and therefore
# spoofable, so it is honored ONLY when the direct peer is a proxy listed
# here; clientIP then walks XFF right→left and takes the first untrusted
# hop as the client.
#
# Unset (default) → X-Forwarded-For is IGNORED and the client IP is the
# direct peer (RemoteAddr): unspoofable, but behind a load balancer / CDN
# every request collapses onto the LB's IP (the per-IP rate limiter becomes
# one global bucket and the audit log records the LB, not the client). So:
#   - directly-exposed deployment → leave unset (correct + safe).
#   - behind a proxy → set this to the proxy's egress CIDR(s), and strip
#     inbound X-Forwarded-For at the edge so a client can't pre-seed it.
# Comma-separated CIDRs (a bare IP is treated as /32 or /128). The literal
# "none" is a synonym for TF_CAPTURE_CLIENT_IP=false. Multi mode only.
# TF_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDR=10.0.0.0/8,192.168.0.0/16

# Capture client IPs at all. Default true. Set false (data minimization)
# to store NULL for sessions.ip_addr and auth_events.ip_address; clientIP
# returns "" and the per-IP rate limiter degrades to one global bucket.
# Boolean (case-insensitive, whitespace-tolerant). Multi mode only.
# TF_CAPTURE_CLIENT_IP=true

# GitHub OAuth app — create one at https://github.com/settings/developers
# with authorized callback URL ${TF_PUBLIC_URL}/auth/v1/callback.
GH_CLIENT_ID=
GH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# GOTRUE_JWT_KEYS, GOTRUE_JWT_SECRET, and TF_GOTRUE_SERVICE_ROLE_TOKEN are all
# written by `triagefactory jwk-init --write-env .env`. They contain secret
# material (private RSA material, a random HS256 fallback secret, and a signed
# admin token) — keep this file off VCS. GOTRUE_JWT_KEYS + GOTRUE_JWT_SECRET are
# read by the gotrue service; TF_GOTRUE_SERVICE_ROLE_TOKEN is read by the TF
# binary (see the SAML SSO block below). jwk-init upserts each var in place
# (replacing the line, collapsing stale duplicates) — re-running is clean.

# --- SAML SSO (Microsoft Entra et al.), multi mode -------------------------
# SSO runs through GoTrue acting as a SAML Service Provider. Connections are
# configured PER-ORG at runtime (an org admin pastes their IdP's metadata URL
# into the in-app "Configure SSO" surface → TF registers it with GoTrue) — there
# is NO env-driven provider here. These two vars are the deployment-wide GoTrue
# SAML infra only, and SSO is OFF by default. Full walkthrough: docs/sso-entra.md.
#
# To enable SSO, do BOTH: (1) uncomment GOTRUE_SAML_ENABLED=true, and (2) set
# GOTRUE_SAML_PRIVATE_KEY. Leaving SAML disabled (the default) lets a fresh
# `docker compose up` boot on GitHub-OAuth login alone — no key required.
#
# GOTRUE_SAML_ENABLED is GoTrue's feature switch. It defaults to false in
# docker-compose.yml (GoTrue does NOT infer it from the key's presence — without
# this flag the SAML routes, including the admin API TF registers providers
# through, aren't mounted). Uncomment to turn SSO on:
# GOTRUE_SAML_ENABLED=true
#
# GOTRUE_SAML_PRIVATE_KEY signs GoTrue's outbound SAML AuthnRequests. It is an
# RSA key (NOT ES256) in DER form, base64-encoded — a DIFFERENT key from the
# session-JWT signing material above. Required only when SAML is enabled (enabled
# + blank key → GoTrue refuses to boot). Generate ONCE and keep it stable:
# rotating it invalidates every registered provider (GoTrue's SP signing identity
# changes). Leave blank when SSO is disabled.
#
# Generate with:
#   openssl genpkey -algorithm rsa -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048 -outform DER | base64 -w0
GOTRUE_SAML_PRIVATE_KEY=

# TF_GOTRUE_SERVICE_ROLE_TOKEN is the RS256 service-role JWT the TF binary
# presents as a static bearer to GoTrue's admin SSO API when an org admin
# registers an IdP connection. `triagefactory jwk-init --write-env .env` mints
# and writes it (signed with the same key as GOTRUE_JWT_KEYS) — you don't set it
# by hand; it's listed here for visibility. TF holds ONLY this token, never the
# signing key, and mints nothing itself (an HS256 token over GOTRUE_JWT_SECRET is
# rejected by GoTrue under RS256 mode — this RS256 token is what it accepts).
# Optional: needed only for SSO. Set TF_PUBLIC_URL before running jwk-init so the
# token's iss matches. Treat as a secret.
#
# Enabling SSO on an already-running deployment? Just re-run
# `triagefactory jwk-init --write-env .env`. Because a GOTRUE_JWT_KEYS already
# exists, jwk-init REUSES it and writes only this token — no keypair rotation,
# so existing sessions keep working. (Use --rotate only to deliberately roll the
# signing key, which forces every user to re-authenticate.)
# TF_GOTRUE_SERVICE_ROLE_TOKEN=

# Session-encryption key (32 random bytes). AES-GCM master key for
# the access/refresh tokens stored at-rest in public.sessions.
# Rotating this key invalidates every existing session (the ciphertext
# can no longer be decrypted) — plan a forced re-auth event when
# rotating.
#
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# (base64 also accepted; hex avoids URL-reserved chars in env interp.)
TF_SESSION_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Cookie-signing secret (32 random bytes). HMAC-SHA256 key for the
# short-lived OAuth state cookie that carries the PKCE verifier +
# CSRF token through the GitHub roundtrip. Rotating this only
# invalidates in-flight OAuth handshakes (10-minute window) — active
# sessions are NOT affected, so rotation here is low-impact.
#
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Kept distinct from TF_SESSION_ENCRYPTION_KEY (rather than derived
# from a single master) so the two rotate independently. Matches the
# skynet/authkit convention.
TF_COOKIE_SECRET=

# Secret-encryption key (32 random bytes). AES-GCM master key for org +
# per-user integration secrets (GitHub App PEM / client-secret / webhook-
# secret, PATs, Jira tokens) stored at-rest in public.org_secrets. TF
# encrypts these app-side and stores opaque ciphertext — they are NOT in
# Supabase Vault / pgsodium, so a Postgres container recreate can no
# longer lose them (TFAC-402).
#
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# (base64 also accepted; hex avoids URL-reserved chars in env interp.)
#
# Kept distinct from TF_SESSION_ENCRYPTION_KEY so the two rotate
# independently — a session-key rotation must not invalidate stored
# integration secrets. Rotating THIS key invalidates every stored secret
# (the ciphertext can no longer be decrypted); operators re-enter them.
TF_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# --- Durable blob storage (object store), multi mode ------------------------
# The blueprint workspace is snapshotted to an S3-compatible object store so
# it outlives the executor that created it. Any S3-protocol backend works —
# SeaweedFS, AWS S3, Cloudflare R2, or Supabase Storage's S3 endpoint — behind
# one client. The binary reads the TF_BLOB_* vars in multi mode and REFUSES TO
# BOOT without TF_BLOB_ENDPOINT and TF_BLOB_BUCKET. Local mode ignores them and
# writes blobs under ~/.triagefactory/blobs — no service, no config, so a
# local install is never affected by anything in this block.
#
# Self-host runs the bundled `seaweedfs` service. Fill in the two creds below;
# the compose stack templates them into SeaweedFS's S3 identity AND feeds them
# to TF's client (one identity, one input), creates the bucket on boot, and
# points TF_BLOB_ENDPOINT at http://seaweedfs:8333 for you. ENDPOINT, BUCKET,
# and REGION all have working defaults — leave them unset for self-host.
#
# Generate each with: openssl rand -hex 32
# (Access key must be >=3 chars, secret >=8 — a 64-char hex satisfies both, and
# hex stays clear of URL/shell-reserved characters AND keeps the creds JSON-safe
# inside SeaweedFS's templated s3.json identity.)
TF_BLOB_ACCESS_KEY=
TF_BLOB_SECRET_KEY=

# SaaS / BYO (point at a hosted Supabase Storage / S3 / R2 bucket instead of
# the bundled SeaweedFS): set the creds above to that backend's keys and
# uncomment the three overrides below. No compose change — it's the same
# client, only the endpoint differs. The `seaweedfs` service still starts but
# goes unused; if you remove the SeaweedFS services in an override, also remove
# `seaweedfs-postinit` from `triagefactory`'s `depends_on`.
#
#   TF_BLOB_ENDPOINT  Full S3 endpoint URL, scheme://host[:port][/base/path].
#                     The scheme selects TLS (https:// vs http://). A base path
#                     IS preserved — point it at S3/R2 (https://host) or
#                     Supabase Storage's path-prefixed S3 endpoint
#                     (https://<ref>.supabase.co/storage/v1/s3).
#                     Self-host default: http://seaweedfs:8333
#   TF_BLOB_BUCKET    Bucket the workspace blobs live in; pre-create it.
#                     Self-host default: tf-workspaces
#   TF_BLOB_REGION    S3 region — wanted by AWS S3 / Supabase, ignored by
#                     SeaweedFS. Self-host default: us-east-1
#
# TF_BLOB_ENDPOINT=
# TF_BLOB_BUCKET=
# TF_BLOB_REGION=

# ── Atlassian OAuth app (first-party, optional) ──────────────────────────────
# The first-party Atlassian OAuth (3LO) app TF uses for one-click "Connect Jira"
# against Cloud orgs. Deployment config in the same class as the GoTrue signing
# material — read from here in multi mode, NOT the keychain/vault, because TF
# registers one first-party Atlassian app for the whole deployment (a distributed
# local binary can't ship a shared client_secret, so local mode ignores these and
# the local user supplies a BYO app from Settings instead).
#
# Leave unset to run without a hosted Atlassian app; each org can still supply its
# own from Workspace Settings → Atlassian OAuth app (a per-org override always
# wins over this default). Register the app in the Atlassian developer console
# (https://developer.atlassian.com/console/myapps/) and set its callback /
# redirect URL to ${TF_PUBLIC_URL}/api/orgs/{org}/jira/connect/callback.
# TF_ATLASSIAN_CLIENT_ID=
# TF_ATLASSIAN_CLIENT_SECRET=
